    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_9a5f25b22bcf6c66a25610714f509a9104f20e4557d7b90570861eeb30c99885",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The source explains Gram structure as a finite-conductor scaffold rather than an asymptotic law: functional-equation data generate the highest Riemann-Siegel tone and initial indexed interlacing, while accumulated S(t) displacement produces long blocks of left-right sloshing that destroy precise indexing even though local one-zero occupancy remains governed by random-matrix spacing.",
